///! std::random: pseudo-random and OS-backed randomness.
///!
///! Everything is built on one trait, `RandomSource` - a type that can
///! produce 64 random bits. From that single primitive come integers,
///! booleans, floats, bounded ranges, byte fills, in-place shuffling,
///! and uniform choice, so generic code written against `R: RandomSource`
///! runs against any generator.
///!
///! Two generators ship:
///!
///!   - `Rng` - a fast, non-cryptographic PRNG (xoshiro256**), seeded
///!     reproducibly (`Rng::from_seed`) or from the OS (`Rng::from_os`).
///!     POD: copying an `Rng` forks the stream. NOT for security.
///!   - `SecureRng` - the kernel CSPRNG (`getrandom`), for keys, tokens,
///!     and nonces. `try_fill` reports entropy failure as a `Result`.
///!
///! `distribution` layers samplers over any source: `UniformU64` /
///! `UniformI64` / `UniformF64`, `Bernoulli`, `Normal`, `Exponential`,
///! and `WeightedIndex`, all reached through the `Distribution<T>` trait.

namespace std::random;

public module random::error;
public module random::bits;
public module random::source;
public module random::rng;
public module random::secure;
public module random::distribution;
